# Line endings for the fixtures whose bytes are asserted (sw-design.md SD33).
#
# `core.autocrlf` is a setting on a *machine*, not in the repository, so
# without an attribute the bytes a fixture checks out with are a property of
# whoever cloned it. Every path below has a test that compares its bytes
# against a generator's output, so leaving them unpinned makes those tests'
# results depend on the runner — which is how CI's Windows leg came to be red
# from M3 onwards while the Linux leg was green on the same commit.
#
# Deliberately narrow, and deliberately **not** `* text=auto eol=lf`: the paths
# named here are the ones a test compares byte-for-byte. Renormalising the
# repository would rewrite every tracked file for no gate's benefit.

# CRLF **by construction**, and `-text` is the only correct attribute.
# `generate_hazards.py` joins every line with CRLF because the delivery it
# stands in for does, and `h13_astrana_blank_row` carries Astrana's doubled
# CRLF (`\r\r\n`) — a sequence no text normalisation survives, because git's
# clean filter folds `\r\n` to `\n` and so rewrites `\r\r\n` as a plain
# `\r\n`, deleting the hazard. `eol=crlf` round-trips through LF and would do
# exactly that, so it is wrong here; `-text` converts in neither direction.
# `diff` is set alongside it so `git diff` still renders them as text.
tests/fixtures/deliveries/hazards/** -text diff

# LF by construction — these generators write JSON and templates with `\n`.
tests/fixtures/codelists/hazards/** eol=lf
tests/fixtures/prompts/hazards/** eol=lf
tests/fixtures/golden/import_report_hazards.json eol=lf
